30
00:00:48,903 --> 00:00:49,793
Back in

31
00:00:49,793 --> 00:00:52,733
1971, an economist called Herbert Simon,

32
00:00:52,733 --> 00:00:54,573
who later won a Nobel Prize,

33
00:00:54,573 --> 00:00:57,213
wrote that a wealth of information creates a

34
00:00:57,213 --> 00:00:58,533
poverty of attention.

35
00:00:58,533 --> 00:01:00,913
What he meant was that information is

36
00:01:00,913 --> 00:01:01,833
everywhere.

37
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:04,373
The thing that has now become scarce is your

38
00:01:04,373 --> 00:01:06,713
attention. And there is only so much of human

39
00:01:06,713 --> 00:01:08,133
attention to go around.

40
00:01:08,133 --> 00:01:10,273
So the attention economy is a system where

41
00:01:10,273 --> 00:01:12,993
human attention is treated as a scarce,

42
00:01:12,993 --> 00:01:15,363
valuable thing and it's traded like currency.

43
00:01:15,363 --> 00:01:17,533
Now think about our social media apps make

44
00:01:17,533 --> 00:01:18,323
their money.

45
00:01:18,323 --> 00:01:20,113
You don't pay Instagram to use Instagram.

46
00:01:20,113 --> 00:01:21,377
The majority of people that use Instagram

47
00:01:21,377 --> 00:01:21,937
don't pay Instagram.

48
00:01:21,937 --> 00:01:22,637
You don't pay TikTok.

49
00:01:22,637 --> 00:01:23,977
You don't pay Facebook.

50
00:01:23,977 --> 00:01:25,837
People call it

51
00:01:25,837 --> 00:01:27,117
a free service,

52
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:29,097
but in a way it is monetised.

53
00:01:29,097 --> 00:01:31,717
They make money from your engagement and your

54
00:01:31,717 --> 00:01:34,687
attention by selling targeted advertisement.

55
00:01:34,687 --> 00:01:37,057
So the more time you spend on the app,

56
00:01:37,057 --> 00:01:38,857
the more ads you see,

57
00:01:38,857 --> 00:01:40,977
the more the app learns about you.

58
00:01:40,977 --> 00:01:43,517
It's your time and your attention that are

59
00:01:43,517 --> 00:01:45,237
actually being sold in a way.

60
00:01:45,237 --> 00:01:47,437
And that's why so many of these apps are

61
00:01:47,437 --> 00:01:49,217
built to keep you in them for as long

62
00:01:49,217 --> 00:01:49,997
as possible.

63
00:01:49,997 --> 00:01:51,467
So your attention

64
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:53,067
is in a way the product.

65
00:01:53,067 --> 00:01:54,827
Now technology can be designed in a way that

66
00:01:54,827 --> 00:01:56,137
changes people's behavior.

67
00:01:56,137 --> 00:01:58,537
And this idea is called persuasive technology.

68
00:01:58,537 --> 00:02:00,507
A lot of it traces back to a researcher

69
00:02:00,507 --> 00:02:02,267
at Stanford University called B.J.

70
00:02:02,267 --> 00:02:02,937
Fogg.

71
00:02:02,937 --> 00:02:05,907
He ran a lab there called the persuasive

72
00:02:05,907 --> 00:02:08,547
technology lab and later published a book on

73
00:02:08,547 --> 00:02:10,947
using computers to change what people think

74
00:02:10,947 --> 00:02:11,627
and do.

75
00:02:11,627 --> 00:02:12,827
To make it more interesting,

76
00:02:12,827 --> 00:02:14,927
in 2007 B.J.

77
00:02:14,927 --> 00:02:15,630
Fogg.

78
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:18,810
taught a famous class at Stanford where

79
00:02:18,810 --> 00:02:21,860
students use these ideas to build

80
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:23,490
apps. Within weeks,

81
00:02:23,490 --> 00:02:25,490
their apps had millions of users.

82
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:27,430
Some of these students went on to build some

83
00:02:27,430 --> 00:02:29,890
of the popular apps that we now know.

84
00:02:29,890 --> 00:02:30,650
B.J. Fogg.

85
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:33,650
also described a simple model of behavior for

86
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:35,040
someone to do something.

87
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:37,330
One, they need motivation.

88
00:02:37,330 --> 00:02:39,590
Two, they need the ability to do it.

89
00:02:39,590 --> 00:02:40,350
And three,

90
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:41,850
they need a prompt.

91
00:02:41,850 --> 00:02:44,180
Now thinking in terms of social apps,

92
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:45,570
think about a notification.

93
00:02:45,570 --> 00:02:47,510
The notification is the prompt.

94
00:02:47,510 --> 00:02:49,470
Opening the app is the ability

95
00:02:49,470 --> 00:02:50,150
to do it.

96
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:51,970
And that just takes a single tap.

97
00:02:51,970 --> 00:02:54,030
So it's an easy ability.

98
00:02:54,030 --> 00:02:55,570
And then the motivation.

99
00:02:55,570 --> 00:02:56,370
The motivation,

100
00:02:56,370 --> 00:02:57,670
especially with social apps,

101
00:02:57,670 --> 00:03:00,120
is the curiosity about what is waiting for you

102
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:01,750
there. And again,

103
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:03,390
some of these people who studied with B.J.

104
00:03:03,390 --> 00:03:04,120
Fogg

105
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:05,690
went on to shape the apps that we use

106
00:03:05,690 --> 00:03:08,090
today. So you can link how everything works

107
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:09,090
together.

108
00:03:09,090 --> 00:03:09,950
Mike Krieger,

109
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:11,170
one of the co-founders of Instagram,

110
00:03:11,170 --> 00:03:12,350
was a student of his.

111
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:13,930
And so was Tristan Harris,

112
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:16,110
who later became one of the loudest critics of

113
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:16,970
this kind of design,

114
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:19,570
actually. This is not to demonise all of the

115
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:21,220
things that these people did or taught.

116
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:23,950
But the ideas that he found and taught were

117
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:24,650
real. And of course,

118
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:26,350
the industry picked up on them and made a

119
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:27,560
shitload of money out of them.

120
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:30,010
The motivation is a very important one.

121
00:03:30,010 --> 00:03:30,720
It's the

122
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:33,240
curiosity that something on that app is

123
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:34,340
waiting for you.

124
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:36,790
Now, the most powerful part of all of this

125
00:03:36,790 --> 00:03:37,580
is the

126
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:38,200
unpredictable

127
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:39,440
reward at the end of it.

128
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:41,300
It is the bedrock of so many things.

129
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:43,080
It is also the bedrock of gambling.

130
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:43,900
For example,

131
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:45,160
you don't know what you will get out of

132
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:46,400
it. Sometimes nothing,

133
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:47,360
sometimes a small thing,

134
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:48,660
sometimes a big thing.

135
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:50,580
The same principle kind of applies to social

136
00:03:50,580 --> 00:03:52,340
media. And psychologists have known this for a

137
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:52,840
long time,

138
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:55,200
that reward that you cannot predict tends to

139
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,350
make you come back strongly,

140
00:03:57,350 --> 00:03:59,340
even more strongly than reward that you can

141
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:00,840
predict. If I know that if I do this

142
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:01,840
thing, I will get this thing,

143
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:03,620
I probably won't come back to it as much

144
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:06,020
as I would if there was an unpredictable

145
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:07,240
reward waiting for me.

146
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:09,320
And this is exactly how your phone works.

147
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:11,260
You don't know if there is a new message,

148
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:12,740
a new like on your video,

149
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:14,000
a new video for you to see,

150
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,110
a new gist.

151
00:04:15,110 --> 00:04:17,000
And that not knowing.

152
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:17,540
It's

153
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,800
exactly why you keep picking up your phone to

154
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:20,400
check.

155
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:22,360
And there's so many designs that are now put

156
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:23,920
in place to make sure that you're

157
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:27,400
constantly on your phone and distracted by it.

158
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:30,060
The first of many is the pull-to-refresh.

159
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:32,440
You know that gesture where you drag your feed

